  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study developed a three-dimensional heat risk framework evaluating hazard, exposure, and vulnerability across 293 prefecture-level cities in China using machine learning and multi-source data. The analysis revealed that 64% of these cities face moderate to high heat risk, with higher risks concentrated in the northwest and lower risks in the southeast. Among the landscape metrics analyzed, average building volume, average height, and the building structure index emerged as key factors, accounting for 17%, 11%, and 10% of the association with heat risk respectively, through distinct nonlinear relationships.",
      "why": "It establishes a scalable, national-scale assessment framework that quantifies how specific, multi-dimensional urban morphology and building structures nonlinearly influence heat risk, moving beyond single-city studies to inform macro-level urban planning."
    }
  },
